>>
endobj
669 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Radon is a naturally occurring radioactive gas formed from the breakdown of uranium in soil, rock, and groundwater. It is colorless, odorless, tasteless, and invisible. While it cannot be seen or detected without testing, it can enter homes and buildings and accumulate indoors. Long-term exposure to elevated radon levels is a leading cause of lung cancer among non-smokers.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
670 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Radon can enter structures through a variety of common pathways, including cracks in concrete slabs, floor-wall joints, gaps around pipes and utility penetrations, sump pits, crawl spaces, construction joints, and other openings in foundations. Even newly constructed homes and buildings can experience elevated radon levels depending on site conditions.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
671 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
672 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency \(EPA\) recommends action when radon levels reach 4.0 picocuries per liter \(pCi/L\) or higher. Long-term exposure at or above this level can increase the risk of lung cancer and affects both smokers and non-smokers. Without testing, radon exposure often goes unnoticed.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
673 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Modern building practices include radon-resistant construction techniques designed to reduce radon entry and make future mitigation easier if needed. These typically include a gas-permeable layer beneath the slab that allows soil gases to move toward a venting system, a minimum 6-mil plastic vapor barrier to help block gas entry, and sealed joints and openings to reduce pathways into the structure.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
674 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Some homes are also built with passive radon systems, which typically include a gravel layer beneath the foundation, a vent pipe that runs through the structure and exits above the roofline, and a rough-in for a future fan installation. If post-construction testing shows elevated levels, these systems can often be upgraded to active mitigation systems by adding a fan.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
675 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Homeowners are encouraged to test their homes if they have a basement or crawl space, have never tested before, or have recently purchased or renovated a property. Regular maintenance can also help reduce radon entry by sealing foundation cracks, maintaining sump covers, keeping vapor barriers intact, and repairing crawl space liners. Retesting is recommended after major renovations, after mitigation work, after HVAC changes, or every few years as part of routine home maintenance.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
